Below is a minimal “recipe” you can drop into your Java‑based ElasticSearch plugin / logger helper class.  
It shows **how** to pull the node‑ID out of an incoming `org.elasticsearch.common.logging.LogEvent`, then hand both pieces of data over to the supplied `NodeIdConverter.NodeIdConverter_format` function so that the node‑ID gets appended to whatever `StringBuilder` (`logBuilder`) you’re already building up.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.logging.LogEvent;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Prepare the objects you’ll feed into the converter.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Your existing StringBuilder – this holds everything else you want logged.
 */
private final StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();

/**
 * A method that receives a LogEvent coming from ES’s internal logging system.
 *
 * @param logEvent   The raw LogEvent produced by ES.
 */
public void handleIncoming(LogEvent logEvent) {

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------
     * 2️⃣  Convert the LogEvent to its textual form.
     *      Most LogEvents expose a formatted message through:
     *          - getMessage()
     *          - getFormattedMessage()
     *      If you prefer the full JSON payload, you could also do:
     *          logEvent.toJson();   // only if such a method exists on your version
     * ------------------------------------------------------------- */

    String eventText = logEvent.getFormattedMessage();

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------
     * 3️⃣  Call the injected conversion routine.
     *      According to the contract defined in the developer section,
     *      the function takes two Strings:
     *        • the serialized LogEvent (“event”) 
     *        • the current contents of the StringBuilder (“toAppendTo”)
     *      It mutates/appends the node‑id onto the second argument.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------- */

    Object result =
            functions.NodeIdConverter_NodeIdConverter_format(
                    Map.of("event", eventText, "toAppendTo", logBuilder.toString()));

    /*
     * NOTE: Because Java's StringBuilder isn’t passed by reference here,
     *       the library implementation most probably returns the mutated
     *       value as a plain String.  Capture that return value and set
     *       it back into our own StringBuilder instance.
     */
    if (result instanceof String) {
        logBuilder.setLength(0);           // clear old content
        logBuilder.append((String) result);
    } else {
        // Fallback: nothing changed – keep original text
        logBuilder.append(eventText);
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------------------
     * 4️⃣  Now you have a fully enriched log line ready to ship somewhere.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------- */
}
```

### What each part does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **Step 1** | You already had a `StringBuilder` called `logBuilder`. That stays untouched until we finish formatting. |
| **Step 2** | Pull the human‑readable portion of the `LogEvent`. Usually `getFormattedMessage()` gives you exactly what you’d see in the console. |
| **Step 3** | Pass the event text plus the current builder contents to the external `format(...)` routine. The routine knows how to look at the event, extract the node‑ID (via the `NodeAndClusterIdStateListener`), and tack it onto the end of the string. |
| **Step 4** | Replace the old builder contents with whatever the formatter returned. |

> ⚠️ **Important note about immutability:**  
> The API definition says the function accepts *Strings*, not actual `StringBuilder`s. Therefore the underlying implementation cannot mutate your `StringBuilder` directly—it has to create a new string containing the appended node‑ID. Hence why we capture the return value and overwrite the builder ourselves.

---

#### Quick sanity check

If you run the above inside a unit test where you mock a `LogEvent` that reports `"node_id":"my-node"` in its metadata, after calling `handleIncoming(myMockedEvent)` you should find `logBuilder.toString()` ending with `... my-node`.

Feel free to tweak the exact field names (`"event"`, `"toAppendTo"`) depending on how the real `NodeIdConverter_nodeIdConverter_format` was wired up—just make sure they match the keys expected by the function.
